window.CS_NARR=window.CS_NARR||{};window.CS_NARR["ovalgo"]={"ovalgoatlas": [[null, ["Hi, Buddy here. This sheet goes from an empty folder to a monitored live trading bot, using the OpenAlgo Python SDK. It's condensed from OpenAlgo's free Open Varsity course. A lot of the cards are code, and I won't read code line by line. Instead I'll tell you what each snippet does and why it matters, so follow along on the card. The course is refreshingly honest that most strategies it tests don't beat buy and hold, and that honesty is the real lesson. This is education, not investment advice."]], ["algo-trading-with-python-topic-atlas", ["Here's why the sheet runs in this order. Every script is fetch data, compute, decide, so foundations and market data come first. Indicators turn data into signals, and the strategy playbook shows which signals to try. Execution teaches placing orders safely. Testing comes before going live, because an untested strategy is a guess. And live trading closes the sheet, with streaming, sizing and the go live checklist."]], ["how-to-use-this-sheet", ["If you're new to Python, do foundations then market data; every later script follows the same shape: fetch a DataFrame, compute, decide. If you're building a strategy, go indicators, then signals, then the playbook, then testing, in that order. Before any real order, read execution, the backtest trust ladder, position sizing and the go live checklist at the end. Keep OpenAlgo in analyze mode, which simulates orders, the whole time you're learning. And because SDK field names can change between versions, print a response once before relying on its shape."]], ["from-idea-to-live-bot", ["This flow goes from idea to live bot. Get data with history and quotes. Compute indicators with openalgo dot ta. Turn them into signals with crossover, exrem and a one bar shift. Backtest in VectorBT with fees and slippage. Validate with walk forward and out of sample data. Size by risk percentage, an ATR stop, and whole lots. Execute in analyze mode first, then with placeorder. And monitor with a WebSocket feed, stops and alerts. Most ideas should die at the validate step. That's the process working, not failing.", "The takeaway: Data, indicators, shifted signals, a costed backtest, walk-forward validation, risk-based size, then analyze mode before live.", "Pause and answer: Which step comes right after a costed backtest?", "⏸", "Answer: Validation: walk-forward and out-of-sample tests."]]], "setup": [[null, ["Section one: foundations. Setup, secrets, the client, and Python essentials."]], ["set-up-once", ["Set up once. Install Python, and on Windows tick \"Add Python to PATH\"; check it with python dash dash version. Install VS Code and its Python extension. Run OpenAlgo locally; its dashboard opens at 127.0.0.1 port 5000, and you copy your API key from its API Key page. Then pip install uv, run uv init to create a project, change into it, and uv add openalgo. Run scripts with uv run python and the file name. The idea is one isolated, reproducible project per idea.", "The takeaway: Python, VS Code, OpenAlgo running locally at 127.0.0.1:5000, an API key, and a uv project with openalgo installed.", "Pause and answer: Where does the local OpenAlgo dashboard open?", "⏸", "Answer: http://127.0.0.1:5000."]], ["keep-secrets-out-of-code", ["Keep secrets out of code. Put your OpenAlgo API key, host, and later the WebSocket URL, in a dot env file. Load it with python dotenv and read values with os dot getenv. Add dot env to your gitignore, and commit a blank dot env example instead. Treat the key like an ATM PIN: never hard code it, never paste it in a chat, and never push it to GitHub. And replace any placeholder default before running.", "The takeaway: Keys live in a git-ignored .env file loaded with python-dotenv; commit only a blank .env.example.", "Pause and answer: How should you treat the API key?", "⏸", "Answer: Like an ATM PIN: never hard-code it, paste it in chat or push it to GitHub."]], ["the-client-analyze-mode-and-the-safe-response-pa", ["The client, analyze mode, and the safe response pattern. The snippet loads the dot env file, creates the API client with the key and host from the environment, and prints the analyzer status, to confirm analyze mode is on while learning. Then it requests a quote for SBIN and checks the status: on success it reads the data and computes the percentage change from the previous close; otherwise it prints the error message. Four lessons. Analyze mode simulates orders, so nothing reaches the exchange; analyzer toggle with mode true switches it on, and going live should be a deliberate act, never an accident. The payload sits one layer down: resp data for most calls, resp results for batch calls, and forgetting that layer is the commonest beginner error. client dot funds gives available cash, utilised debits and realised and unrealised MTM, and client dot intervals lists supported timeframes. And wrap every live call in try and except, so one bad reply can't kill the bot. An unknown symbol returns an HTTP 400 \"not found\", so check the master contracts are downloaded.", "The takeaway: Create the client from environment variables, confirm analyze mode while learning, and check status before reading data.", "Pause and answer: Why check analyzerstatus() at startup while learning?", "⏸", "Answer: To confirm orders go to the sandbox, not the live market."]], ["python-essentials-that-carry-the-course", ["The Python essentials that carry the course. Lists hold watchlists, indexed from 0, with minus 1 as the latest. Dicts are every SDK reply, read with nested keys like data then ltp. f strings align output. For, if and def scale one check to many symbols, branch on data, and avoid repetition. Try and except keep a bot alive through errors. And comprehensions compute things like day over day changes in one line. Profit and loss is exit minus entry, times quantity, reversed for shorts. A long from 1,300 to 1,345 on 50 shares makes ₹2,250. A short from 1,300 to 1,280 on 50 makes ₹1,000. And 750 SBIN at 812.4 with a last price of 818 is up ₹4,200.", "The takeaway: Lists for watchlists, dicts for every SDK reply, f-strings for output, loops and functions to scale, try/except to stay alive.", "Pause and answer: How do you read the LTP from a quotes reply?", "⏸", "Answer: resp[\"data\"][\"ltp\"]."]]], "data": [[null, ["Section two: market data. Symbols, quotes, history, NumPy and pandas."]], ["one-symbol-format-for-every-broker", ["OpenAlgo uses one symbol format for every broker. Equities use the base name, like RELIANCE. Futures are the base, then the date as day, month and year, then FUT, like NIFTY 30 JUN 26 FUT. Options are the base, date, strike, then CE or PE. Commodity futures follow the futures format. The exchange codes: NSE and BSE for stocks, NFO and BFO for futures and options, MCX for commodities, which trade into the evening, and NSE underscore INDEX for quote only indices like the Nifty and India VIX. Always pair a symbol with its exchange. Asking for the Nifty on NSE instead of NSE INDEX, or a future on NSE instead of NFO, are classic misses.", "The takeaway: OpenAlgo uses one symbol format across brokers: base, then DDMMMYY, strike and CE/PE for options, FUT for futures.", "Pause and answer: Format a NIFTY 24,000 call expiring 30 June 2026.", "⏸", "Answer: NIFTY30JUN2624000CE."]], ["discovering-instruments-and-lot-sizes", ["Discovering instruments and lot sizes. client dot symbol returns details for one instrument, including lot size, tick size, type and expiry. search finds matching instruments, like Nifty futures on NFO. expiry lists the expiries for options. And instruments returns the whole master as a DataFrame, about 9,700 rows for NSE. Contract value is lot size times the last price. Sample lot sizes: Nifty 65, Bank Nifty 30, the Reliance future 500, and commodity minis like Gold M, crude and Silver M at 1. Ticks: SBIN 0.05, Reliance 0.1, the Bank Nifty future 0.2, crude 1. Exchanges revise lots, so look them up every time and never hard code them. One Nifty futures lot is about 65 times 23,810, about ₹15.5 lakh. And with weekly index options, one strike has many expiries; the nearest is usually the most active.", "The takeaway: symbol(), search() and expiry() return lot size, tick size and expiries, so nothing is hard-coded.", "Pause and answer: Which call returns a symbol's lot size and tick size?", "⏸", "Answer: client.symbol(...)."]], ["quotes-multiquotes-and-depth", ["Quotes, multiquotes and depth. quotes returns one snapshot: last price, open, high, low, previous close, bid, ask, volume and open interest. multiquotes takes a list of symbols across mixed exchanges and returns a result per item in one request; use it beyond two or three symbols. depth returns the order book: last price, total buy and sell quantity, and five levels of bids and asks. Useful formulas: change percent is last price minus previous close, over previous close, times 100. Spread is best ask minus best bid, for Gold M about 22 points, roughly 0.015%. Buy pressure is buy quantity over total quantity. And range position is where the last price sits between the day's low and high. NSE and BSE cash trade 9:15 to 3:30, and when the market is shut depth is empty and quotes can show zeros, so guard against an empty bid list and zero division. Practise depth on MCX in the evening. Check the spread before trading anything unfamiliar, and treat imbalance and OI as supporting clues, never triggers on their own.", "The takeaway: quotes for one snapshot, multiquotes for many symbols in one request, depth for the order book.", "Pause and answer: When should you switch from quotes to multiquotes?", "⏸", "Answer: Beyond 2–3 symbols: one request for many."]], ["historical-candles", ["Historical candles. The snippet builds a start date 120 days ago, then calls history for TCS on NSE with a daily interval, formatting the dates as year, month, day. It returns a DataFrame indexed by timestamp, with open, high, low, close, volume and OI. The intervals are 1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 30 and 60 minutes, 1 hour, and daily. Ten calendar days of TCS gave 7 daily rows, 49 hourly, 175 fifteen minute and 525 five minute rows. Calendar days aren't sessions: 7 days gave 6 rows, 30 gave 21, and 90 gave 59, so ask for a generous window and count rows. Intraday timestamps carry the plus 5:30 Indian offset; the first 5 minute bar is 9:15 and the last 3:25. OI is zero for stocks. source equals db reads OpenAlgo's local store instead of the broker, so wrap it in try except. And build dates from datetime so scripts never go stale.", "The takeaway: history() returns a DataFrame indexed by timestamp, with open, high, low, close and volume.", "Pause and answer: What does client.history() return?", "⏸", "Answer: A pandas DataFrame indexed by timestamp."]], ["resample-the-right-way", ["Resample the right way. Define an OHLCV recipe: open takes the first value, high the max, low the min, close the last, and volume the sum. Resampling daily data with \"W\" and that recipe gives weekly bars; \"ME\" gives month end. Resampling 5 minute data to 60 minutes gives hourly bars. Save to CSV for reproducible tests and read it back with the index parsed as dates. Never average OHLC bars; first, max, min, last and sum is the only recipe. And resampling only goes up, from 5 minutes to an hour, never back, so download the smallest interval you'll need.", "The takeaway: Resample OHLCV with first, max, min, last and sum; save CSVs for reproducible tests.", "Pause and answer: Which aggregation does volume use when resampling?", "⏸", "Answer: Sum."]], ["numpy-in-five-moves", ["NumPy in five moves. One, arrays: np dot array, np dot arange, which excludes the end, and np dot linspace; check shape, ndim, size and dtype, and prices are float 64. Two, indexing: minus 1 is the latest, a slice from 1 to 4 is three items, minus 5 colon is the last five, and in 2D, colon comma minus 1 is the last column. Three, vectorise: multiply the whole array by 1.05 or by quantity, with no Python loops over prices. Four, aggregate: mean, max, min, std, median, sum and cumsum, with axis 1 across rows and axis 0 down columns. Five, masks: closes greater than their mean gives a True and False array; index with it, sum it to count, or use np dot where for 1 or 0, which is the seed of every signal. With five Reliance closes from 1,305 to 1,312.7, the mean is 1,309.04, the standard deviation 6.84, and three days are above average.", "The takeaway: Create arrays, index and slice, vectorise maths, aggregate, and use boolean masks: no loops over prices.", "Pause and answer: Does np.arange(1, 6) include 6?", "⏸", "Answer: No."]], ["pandas-for-price-series", ["pandas for price series. Picking a column with single brackets gives a Series; double brackets give a DataFrame. iloc minus 1 gives the latest row and loc with a date slices from that date; keep the timestamp index, because it powers dates, resampling and weekdays. Rolling windows compute mean, max, min and std, and the first 19 values of a 20 bar window are NaN by design. Returns come from pct change, and growth of ₹1 lakh is one plus returns, cumulative product, times 1 lakh; the first return is NaN. Drawdown is close over its running maximum, minus one, and its minimum is the maximum drawdown, the core risk number. You can group by weekday name, concatenate two stocks side by side, and correlate their returns, not their prices. Describe summarises, and annual volatility is daily standard deviation times the square root of 252. A \"best weekday\" from a few months is noise. And check shape and head on every new download before doing any maths.", "The takeaway: Keep the timestamp index; single brackets give a Series, double a DataFrame; rolling windows start with NaNs.", "Pause and answer: df[\"close\"] versus df[[\"close\"]]?", "⏸", "Answer: A Series versus a one-column DataFrame."]]], "viz": [[null, ["Section three: charts and apps, with Matplotlib, Plotly and Streamlit."]], ["matplotlib-precise-charts-saved-to-png", ["Matplotlib makes precise charts saved as PNG. Create a figure and axis with subplots, draw with plot, bar, hist, scatter, fill between and axhline, and finish with savefig at 110 dpi and tight borders, so it runs headless with no window. To avoid weekend gaps, plot against a simple counter and label every Nth tick with the formatted date. For price over volume, use two rows sharing the x axis with height ratios of 2 to 1. You can overlay SMAs and EMAs from openalgo dot ta, mark buys and sells with up and down triangles, hand build candles from a wick line and a body bar, draw an equity curve with a line at 1.0, fill a drawdown to zero, and rebase stocks to 100 for comparison. Seaborn adds statistics: return histograms, correlation heatmaps built from returns not prices, and seasonality grids. And remember, markers sit on the signal bar's close, but a backtest must act on the next bar.", "The takeaway: Use fig, ax with savefig for headless PNGs, and plot against a range to remove weekend gaps.", "Pause and answer: How do you remove weekend gaps on a price chart?", "⏸", "Answer: Plot against range(len(df)) and label ticks with dates."]], ["plotly-interactive-candles", ["Plotly makes interactive candles. The snippet formats dates as day and month labels, creates a two row figure sharing the x axis, with 70% of the height for price and 30% for volume. It adds a candlestick trace to row 1 and a volume bar trace to row 2, sets the x axis type to category to remove weekend gaps, hides the range slider, and writes the chart to an HTML file you can open offline. The vocabulary: a figure is the chart, a trace is one series, and the layout holds axes and titles. Overlays in price units share the price pane, while bounded indicators like RSI and MACD get their own pane, with horizontal lines at 70 and 30. Set row and col on every trace. And write image needs the kaleido package and loses interactivity.", "The takeaway: make_subplots with a candlestick row and a volume row gives an interactive, zoomable chart.", "Pause and answer: Which Plotly object draws candles?", "⏸", "Answer: go.Candlestick."]], ["streamlit-a-dashboard-in-one-script", ["Streamlit builds a dashboard in one script. The snippet caches a history loader for 60 seconds, takes a symbol from a text input and an exchange from a select box, loads the data, shows the last close as a metric, and draws a line chart of closes. Launch it with streamlit run, not python, and it opens on localhost port 8501. The key mental model: the whole script re runs from the top whenever a widget changes. So write it as if it runs once, and cache every network call, or each slider move refetches history. Plain variables don't persist between runs. The building blocks are title, metric, table, line chart and plotly chart, plus widgets like text input, select box and slider. A watchlist board is one multiquotes call, and a scanner is a loop of history plus RSI.", "The takeaway: Streamlit turns a script into a dashboard; cache data with a TTL matched to how fast it changes.", "Pause and answer: What TTL suits quotes versus daily history?", "⏸", "Answer: Seconds for quotes, hours for daily history."]]], "ind": [[null, ["Section four: indicators with openalgo dot ta."]], ["know-the-return-type-before-you-index", ["Know the return type before you index. Many functions return a pandas Series, including the moving averages, RSI, CCI, Williams percent R, ATR, OBV, VWAP and others; read the latest with iloc minus 1. Others return a tuple of Series: MACD returns three, Bollinger, Keltner and Donchian return three bands, Supertrend two, Ichimoku five, ADX three, pivot points seven, and so on; unpack them first, for example m, s, h equals ta dot macd. And the utility functions, crossover, crossunder, highest, lowest, exrem, flip, valuewhen, rising, falling and roc, return a NumPy array; read with minus 1, or wrap it in a Series with the DataFrame's index. Mixing these up, calling iloc on an array, unpacking a Series, or assigning a tuple to one name, is the most common indicator bug.", "The takeaway: Some indicators return a Series, others a tuple of Series; check before indexing.", "Pause and answer: What does ta.macd return?", "⏸", "Answer: A tuple of three Series: MACD, signal, histogram."]], ["trend-tools", ["Trend tools. SMA, EMA, WMA and HMA take a close and a length: price above the average is an uptrend, and less lag means more whipsaws, with HMA hugging price. VWMA is volume weighted and needs volume. KAMA adapts: fast in clean trends, slow in chop. Supertrend, with period 10 and multiplier 3, returns a line and a direction, where minus 1 means uptrend and plus 1 downtrend in this library. Ichimoku: above the cloud is bullish, below bearish, inside stand aside. Parabolic SAR takes high and low, not close: a dot below price is bullish, and it doubles as a trailing stop. MA envelopes give a fixed percentage band showing how stretched price is. And the alligator: lips above teeth above jaw means trending up, while tangled lines mean a range. Use periods of 9 or 20 for trading and 50, 100 or 200 for the big picture; the 200 day is the classic bull and bear line. A simple trend filter: poll price above the 50 EMA, Supertrend up, and price above SAR, and lean long only when two of three agree.", "The takeaway: Moving averages, KAMA and Supertrend: less lag means more whipsaws.", "Pause and answer: Supertrend direction −1 means what in openalgo.ta?", "⏸", "Answer: Uptrend."]], ["momentum-tools", ["Momentum tools. RSI 14: above 70 overbought, below 30 oversold, and 50 is the bull and bear line. MACD returns the MACD, signal and histogram: MACD above signal is bullish, and the histogram shows momentum building or fading. The stochastic returns percent K and percent D, with 80 and 20 as zones. CCI is unbounded: above plus 100 is strong up, below minus 100 strong down. Williams percent R runs 0 to minus 100: above minus 20 overbought, below minus 80 oversold. The Fisher transform returns a value and a trigger; look for a cross after an extreme beyond plus or minus 1.5. Connors RSI is for short term mean reversion, below 10 or above 90. And balance of power runs minus 1 to plus 1, showing who won the bar. Zones are context, not triggers: overbought can stay overbought in a strong trend. Divergence, price making a higher high while RSI makes a lower high, is a warning, so wait for confirmation.", "The takeaway: RSI, MACD, stochastic, CCI and Williams %R each have their own zones.", "Pause and answer: RSI zones?", "⏸", "Answer: Above 70 overbought, below 30 oversold, 50 as the bull/bear line."]], ["volatility-tools", ["Volatility tools. ATR 14 is the unit of risk in price: a stop at price minus 2 times ATR. NATR is ATR as a percentage of price, for comparing and sizing across assets. Bollinger Bands are the SMA plus and minus 2 standard deviations; a band touch flags a stretch, not a reversal. Bollinger percent B and bandwidth show where price sits in the band, and a squeeze is when width falls to 1.1 times its 60 day low. Keltner channels are an EMA plus and minus ATR, and Bollinger inside Keltner means compression. Donchian gives N bar high and low breakout levels; 55 is the Turtle setting. The chandelier exit gives trailing long and short stops from the extreme. And historical volatility gives an annualised percentage; HV 20 above HV 60 means volatility is expanding. For Reliance, ATR 14 was 27.52, so a stop from 1,306 sits at about 1,251, risking ₹55 a share, with NATR of 2.11%. Volatility gives you size and range, never direction, and it mean reverts: calm follows storms.", "The takeaway: ATR is the unit of risk, NATR compares across assets, Bollinger bands flag stretch and squeezes.", "Pause and answer: A 2-ATR stop formula?", "⏸", "Answer: Price − 2 × ATR."]], ["volume-tools", ["Volume tools. OBV and the accumulation distribution line are cumulative, so read the slope and divergences, not the level. Chaikin money flow runs minus 1 to plus 1, and beyond plus or minus 0.2 is strong. MFI is a volume weighted RSI, with 80 and 20 zones, best in ranges. Force index: a fresh zero line cross suggests a possible shift. Ease of movement shows how easily price moved per unit of volume. VWAP is session anchored and works on intraday candles only. Relative volume above 1.5 makes breakouts more trustworthy, below 0.7 is quiet. And volume rate of change at plus or minus 50% shows a surge or fade in participation. The workflow: a price event, then participation with RVOL and VROC, then money flow with OBV, ADL or CMF, and act when they agree. Note that index spot has no real volume, so use index futures on NFO.", "The takeaway: OBV and A/D are cumulative (read slope and divergence); CMF beyond ±0.20 is strong; MFI is a volume-weighted RSI.", "Pause and answer: What do you read on OBV?", "⏸", "Answer: Slope and divergences, not the level."]], ["oscillators-check-the-regime-first", ["Oscillators: check the regime first. The choppiness index above 61.8 means choppy, so use range tools; below 38.2 means trending, so use trend tools. CMO has extremes at plus and minus 50. The ultimate oscillator blends 7, 14 and 28 periods, with 70 and 30 zones. Stochastic RSI uses 80 and 20 but whipsaws in trends. The Aroon oscillator near plus or minus 100 means a fresh trend, near 0 none. ROC, TRIX, the awesome oscillator, PPO and TSI are unbounded: which side of zero they're on, and their crosses, confirm trend strength, and PPO compares across symbols. DPO exposes cycles but isn't a direction signal. The course's rule: never trade an oscillator blind to the regime. Bounded oscillators suit ranges; unbounded ones confirm trends. And note that roc returns a NumPy array.", "The takeaway: Choppiness above 61.8 means range tools, below 38.2 means trend tools.", "Pause and answer: CHOP reads 65. Which tools?", "⏸", "Answer: Range tools: it's choppy."]], ["statistical-and-hybrid-tools", ["Statistical and hybrid tools. Linear regression, its slope, and the time series forecast give a regression fair value, its direction, positive meaning up, and a one bar projection. Correlation and beta measure pair strength and market sensitivity, where a beta of 1 tracks the index. Variance and median give swing size and a spike resistant centre. ADX returns plus DI, minus DI and ADX: ADX above 25 is strong, below 20 weak, and the larger DI gives direction. Aroon gives up and down from 0 to 100. Pivot points give the pivot and three resistance and support levels. And fractals flag swing highs and lows. Align date ranges before computing beta or correlation, and sanity check odd outputs; the course flags a suspiciously low beta in one run.", "The takeaway: Linear regression, correlation, beta and ADX describe fair value, relationships, market sensitivity and trend strength.", "Pause and answer: A beta of 1 means what?", "⏸", "Answer: The stock tracks the index."]], ["utility-functions-from-indicator-to-event", ["Utility functions turn indicators into events. crossover and crossunder are True only on the bar of the cross. highest and lowest are rolling max and min, giving breakout levels. exrem drops repeat signals until the other side fires. flip gives 1 from entry until exit, an in position flag. valuewhen gives the value of something at the latest true bar, like the entry price. And rising and falling compare with n bars ago. Combine arrays with ampersand, pipe and tilde, with parentheses, never with the words and and or. And a subtle trap: highest of the high over 20 bars at minus 1 already includes today, so a breakout test against it can never fire. Compare with minus 2, yesterday's level.", "The takeaway: crossover marks the bar of the cross, exrem removes repeats, flip builds an in-position flag.", "Pause and answer: What does exrem do?", "⏸", "Answer: Drops repeat signals until the opposite side fires, so buys and sells alternate."]]], "signals": [[null, ["Section five: signals and scanning."]], ["the-signal-pipeline", ["The signal pipeline. Fetch history. Compute indicators. Write raw conditions, like close above the 50 EMA and RSI above 50. Find turning points with crossover or crossunder. De duplicate with exrem so buys and sells alternate. Shift one bar. And hand entries and exits, as boolean Series, to the backtester. Think of a signal as one True or False answer per bar, aligned to timestamps, not a single decision.", "The takeaway: Fetch, compute, write conditions, find crosses, de-duplicate with exrem, shift one bar, hand to the backtester.", "Pause and answer: Why shift signals one bar?", "⏸", "Answer: To avoid look-ahead: you can act only after the bar that created the signal."]], ["ema-crossover-done-properly", ["An EMA crossover, done properly. The snippet computes a 20 and a 50 EMA, and defines a small helper that wraps an array in a Series with the close's index. Buys are where the fast crosses over the slow, sells where it crosses under. exrem then cleans each so buys and sells alternate. Entries and exits are the buys and sells shifted one bar, so you act on the next bar. flip gives an in position flag that's 1 while holding, and valuewhen gives the price at the last buy. Why exrem matters: in an RSI example, raw buys went from 7 to 2 and raw sells from 47 to 3 after exrem. Without it, you stack repeated buys before any sell.", "The takeaway: Crossover and crossunder, exrem both ways, then shift(1).fillna(False) for entries.", "Pause and answer: Why wrap ta outputs in pd.Series with the close index?", "⏸", "Answer: So signals align with dates for shifting and backtesting."]], ["look-ahead-the-silent-backtest-killer", ["Look ahead is the silent backtest killer. A signal computed from today's close only exists after the close. Trading on that same close assumes you knew the future. The fix is to shift the signal by one bar, for crossovers, RSI levels and breakouts alike, and to compare breakouts with the previous bar's level. Remove the shift and an untradeable backtest becomes a flattering one. The same applies to charts: markers sit on the signal bar's close.", "The takeaway: A signal from today's close exists only after the close; trading that close assumes you knew the future.", "Pause and answer: How do you fix look-ahead for a breakout?", "⏸", "Answer: Compare with the previous bar's level and shift the signal one bar."]], ["scanner-universe-loop-rule", ["A scanner is a universe, a loop and a rule. The snippet loops over a universe of symbols, fetching daily history for each inside try and except, and skipping any symbol that fails or returns an empty frame, because one bad symbol must not kill the scan. For each, it records RSI, whether the close is above the 50 EMA, whether today's close breaks above yesterday's 20 day high, relative volume against the 20 day average, and the 20 day return. Then it builds a board sorted by 20 day return, with 1 based ranks. Typical filters: RSI below 30 or above 70, close above the 50 EMA, a 20 day breakout against yesterday's high, and RVOL above 1.5. For a live snapshot of many names, one multiquotes call beats a loop; loop history only when you need time series indicators. The same loop works for NFO and MCX, whose futures symbols carry the expiry.", "The takeaway: A scanner loops a universe through one rule, skipping symbols that error or return empty data.", "Pause and answer: Why wrap each symbol's fetch in try/except?", "⏸", "Answer: One bad symbol must not kill the whole scan."]]], "play": [[null, ["Section six: the strategy playbook. Equity, intraday, pairs, momentum, seasonal and options."]], ["cash-and-equity-strategies", ["Cash and equity strategies, with the course's honest results. The golden cross enters when the 50 SMA crosses above the 200 and exits on the death cross, trading only in a bullish regime. It needs about 400 calendar days of data or it silently does nothing, and Reliance's regime was bearish, with the 50 SMA at 1,347 against the 200 at 1,421. The 20 and 50 EMA crossover on ICICI Bank lost 15.5%, against minus 7.8% for buy and hold. Momentum rotation ranks 12 NSE stocks by 60 bar return, holds the top 3 and re ranks monthly; leaders were L and T up 24.8% and Axis Bank up 16%, with Infosys the laggard at minus 16.4%. And a Donchian 20 breakout on L and T lost 3.8%, against plus 15.8% buy and hold. The pattern underneath them all: the position is a flip of buys and sells, the strategy return is the daily return times the position shifted by one, and the equity curve is its cumulative product. Delivery trades use product CNC, and guard orders with a regime check so the bot stays flat in downtrends. These cost free previews are a smell test: if a rule can't beat buy and hold, its complexity isn't earning its keep.", "The takeaway: Golden cross, EMA crossover and similar cash strategies need enough history and a regime filter.", "Pause and answer: Why can a golden-cross strategy silently do nothing?", "⏸", "Answer: It needs about 400 calendar days of data for the 200-day average."]], ["intraday-strategies", ["Intraday strategies. The opening range breakout takes the high and low from 9:15 to 9:29 and enters on a later close beyond it, roughly between 9:30 and 3:15; a 15 minute range is faster, while 30 minutes gives fewer fakeouts. VWAP reversion buys when price is stretched below the session VWAP by a threshold, 0.40% in the course, on range days only, with a trend filter to switch it off. A 9 and 21 EMA cross on 5 minute bars trades cross up and cross down. And a time exit stops new entries and forces flat after a cutoff, 3:10 in the course. There are about 75 five minute bars in an NSE session; split days with groupby on the normalised date, and windows with between time. Equity MIS orders are rejected after about 3:15, while MCX runs into the evening, and you should never carry MIS into the close. The result: a Nifty future, 5 minute 9 and 21 EMA, in VectorBT with 0.03% fees, lost 2.08% over 32 trades with 28.1% winners. Costs dominate intraday.", "The takeaway: Opening range breakout, VWAP reversion and intraday EMA trades each need time windows and the right market type.", "Pause and answer: Opening range in the course?", "⏸", "Answer: The 9:15–9:29 high and low; enter on a later close beyond it."]], ["pairs-and-statistical-arbitrage", ["Pairs and statistical arbitrage. The spread is either A divided by B, or A minus beta times B, with beta from a straight line fit of A on B. The z score is the spread minus its 30 day mean, divided by its 30 day standard deviation. When z is above plus 2, sell A and buy B; below minus 2, buy A and sell B; exit when z is back inside plus or minus 0.5; and trade only if return correlation is at least about 0.6. For ICICI Bank and HDFC Bank, return correlation was 0.601, beta 0.597, and the latest z 1.26, but the rolling 30 day correlation fell as low as minus 0.097. So monitor it and stand aside when it breaks. Correlate returns, not prices; size the legs by beta, not one for one; and count both legs' costs, borrow fees and squeeze risk. Spreads can stay dislocated longer than your capital lasts. The course's single leg backtest lost 3.18% over 6 trades. Cointegration is covered properly in the Statistical Arbitrage sheet.", "The takeaway: Trade z-score extremes of a hedged spread, exit near zero, and only when correlation stays healthy.", "Pause and answer: z above +2: which legs?", "⏸", "Answer: Sell A, buy B."]], ["momentum-and-volatility-trading", ["Momentum and volatility trading. Cross sectional momentum ranks by 60 day return and holds the top 3; skipping the latest week, the \"12 minus 1\" approach, is a refinement. An ATR breakout enters when the close exceeds the previous close plus 1 ATR, or 1.5 on Gold M, with inputs shifted a bar. A Bollinger squeeze, bandwidth in the bottom 20% of its 60 day range, gives timing, not direction. A regime filter goes long only when the close is above the 200 day SMA. And volatility targeting sizes the position at a 15% target divided by realised volatility, the 20 day standard deviation times root 252, capped at 3 times. SBIN's realised volatility of 15.4% gives a size of 0.97 times, about 190 shares on ₹2 lakh. A momentum plus regime backtest on ICICI Bank lost 9.76% over 8 trades with an 11.3% maximum drawdown. So judge momentum on a rebalanced basket, not one stock over one year.", "The takeaway: Rank by 60-day return and hold the top names; ATR breakouts and Bollinger squeezes time entries.", "Pause and answer: What does '12-1' momentum skip?", "⏸", "Answer: The most recent period (the latest week or month)."]], ["seasonal-and-calendar-effects", ["Seasonal and calendar effects, tested on about 610 Nifty sessions. Day of week: Wednesday averaged a small gain and Tuesday a small loss, but each weekday has only about 120 samples, which is thin. Month: April, the start of the Indian financial year, averaged a strong gain and January a loss, but that's one reading per year. The turn of the month effect, popular folklore, wasn't supported: those days did slightly worse. Expiry Tuesdays were slightly negative, with roughly even odds of an up day. A rule of trading only on Mondays and Wednesdays beat buy and hold, but the days were chosen on the same data, so it proves nothing. The exact averages are on the card. The method matters more: measure on returns, always print the sample counts, check a year by month heatmap for averages driven by one outlier, and look up the current expiry weekday at runtime with client dot expiry rather than hard coding it.", "The takeaway: Calendar effects in about 610 sessions were small and mostly folklore; samples of about 120 days are thin.", "Pause and answer: Was the turn-of-month effect supported?", "⏸", "Answer: No: slightly negative versus positive otherwise."]], ["options-through-the-sdk", ["Options through the SDK. expiry returns the expiry list. optionchain returns the underlying price, the at the money strike, and a chain with prices for each side. optionsymbol resolves offsets like ATM, ITM n or OTM n to an actual symbol and lot size. optiongreeks returns days to expiry, IV, delta, gamma, theta and vega. syntheticfuture returns the synthetic future price, the ATM strike and spot. And optionsorder and optionsmultiorder place a single leg by offset, or multiple legs with an overall status plus per leg results. Order endpoints want the date as day, short month and two digit year in capitals, so convert with strftime and upper. Discover the nearest expiry at runtime and resolve strikes by offset instead of hard coding. A live run showed the Nifty at 23,824.1, ATM 23,800, the call at 173.35 and the put at 159.45; the ATM call had delta 0.517, theta minus 12.1, vega 13 and IV 12.78%, and the synthetic future was 23,813.9, a basis of minus 10.2. The course codes a long call and put, a bull call spread buying ATM and selling OTM 3, a long straddle, and a protective put. Quantity must be a lot multiple, use NRML to carry, check analyze mode first, and check every leg's status, not just the overall one.", "The takeaway: expiry, optionchain and optionsymbol resolve expiries, the ATM strike and offsets such as ITM2 or OTM3.", "Pause and answer: What does optionsymbol resolve?", "⏸", "Answer: Option symbols from offsets like ATM, ITMn or OTMn."]]], "exec": [[null, ["Section seven: execution. Placing and managing orders."]], ["order-parameters", ["Order parameters. Action is BUY or SELL. Product is CNC for delivery, MIS for intraday, which is auto squared off, or NRML for F and O carried overnight. Price type is MARKET with no price, LIMIT with a price, SL with a price and trigger price, or SL M with just a trigger price. The snippet places a limit buy for 1 SBIN share at 810 for delivery, under a strategy name, and prints the status and order ID. A spelling trap: it's price underscore type in placeorder, placesmartorder and splitorder, but pricetype with no underscore inside basket legs. And match the product to your holding period.", "The takeaway: Action, product (CNC, MIS, NRML), price type (MARKET, LIMIT, SL, SL-M), with price or trigger as needed.", "Pause and answer: Which product is auto squared off intraday?", "⏸", "Answer: MIS."]], ["order-methods", ["Order methods. placeorder sends one order. placesmartorder trades only the difference to an absolute target position, and a target of 0 flattens. basketorder sends many orders at once with per leg results. splitorder chunks a large order under the exchange freeze quantity, so 5 split by 2 becomes 2, 2 and 1. modifyorder re sends full details for an open order, but can't flip a buy to a sell. cancelorder cancels one order, and cancelallorder cancels every pending order for a strategy. And closeposition market closes everything with no confirmation: it's an emergency tool.", "The takeaway: placeorder for one, placesmartorder for a target position, basketorder for many, splitorder above freeze limits.", "Pause and answer: placesmartorder with position_size=0 does what?", "⏸", "Answer: Flattens the position."]], ["which-order-call", ["This ladder picks the right order call. First: do you want to reach a target position, rather than add to one? If yes, use placesmartorder, where position size is the absolute target and 0 flattens. If not: are there several symbols or option legs at once? If yes, use basketorder or optionsmultiorder, then check every leg's status. If not: is the quantity above the exchange freeze limit? If yes, use splitorder, with child orders of splitsize each. Otherwise, use placeorder with the right price type and product.", "The takeaway: Target a position → smart order; many legs → basket; above freeze → split; otherwise placeorder.", "Pause and answer: You want to reach exactly 100 shares regardless of what you hold. Which call?", "⏸", "Answer: placesmartorder with position_size=100."]], ["reading-the-books", ["Reading the books. orderbook returns a dict with an orders list and statistics, including order status and totals of completed, open and rejected orders. tradebook returns a list of fills with action, symbol and average price. positionbook returns a list with symbol, quantity, last price and profit and loss; open positions are those with non zero quantity. And funds returns a dict with available cash and realised and unrealised MTM. The shapes differ, dict versus list, so print each once before writing code against it. NSE cash closes at 3:30 and MIS is rejected after the broker's square off time, so MCX is the evening practice ground. And never wire closeposition to a hair trigger.", "The takeaway: orderbook, tradebook, positionbook and funds show orders, fills, open positions and cash.", "Pause and answer: How do you find open positions in positionbook?", "⏸", "Answer: Rows with quantity not equal to 0."]]], "test": [[null, ["Section eight: testing. Backtests, metrics, optimisation and walk forward. This is the most important section for not fooling yourself."]], ["backtest-by-hand-first", ["Backtest by hand first. The snippet computes a 10 and 30 EMA, sets the signal to 1 when fast is above slow, and shifts it one bar so you trade on yesterday's decision. It computes daily returns, builds an equity curve from ₹1 lakh as the cumulative product of one plus position times return, and a buy and hold curve to compare against, always. The result for SBIN, 10 and 30 EMA, no costs: plus 12.71%, against plus 28.75% for buy and hold. Positive is not the same as good.", "The takeaway: Position = yesterday's signal; equity = cumulative product of 1 + position × return; always compare with buy-and-hold.", "Pause and answer: Why use signal.shift(1) as the position?", "⏸", "Answer: Today you hold what yesterday's signal decided."]], ["vectorbt-with-costs", ["VectorBT with costs. The snippet defines entries as the bar where fast crosses above slow, and exits where it crosses below, then builds a portfolio from those signals with ₹1 lakh of starting cash, 0.1% fees, 0.05% slippage, daily frequency, and long only. Then it reads total return, trade count, maximum drawdown and full stats. SBIN with costs returned plus 10.87% over 6 trades, with about ₹1,136 of fees, a maximum drawdown of about 19.7%, 20% winners and a Sharpe of about 0.75. Going both long and short lost 6.25%. The same settings on Gold M with a 20 and 50 EMA lost 11.43%, against minus 3.05% buy and hold, with a 32% drawdown. Results don't transfer between instruments.", "The takeaway: from_signals with fees and slippage; costs often flip the winner.", "Pause and answer: Fees and slippage used in the course example?", "⏸", "Answer: 0.1% fees and 0.05% slippage."]], ["performance-metrics", ["Performance metrics, using a losing Reliance run as the example. Total return ignores time, so only compare tests of equal length. CAGR, the compound annual growth rate, is the fair yardstick across lengths. Sharpe is the average daily return divided by its standard deviation, scaled by the square root of 252: below zero is losing, about 1 is good, 2 excellent, and 3 or more suspicious. Sortino is like Sharpe but only counts downside volatility. Maximum drawdown is the deepest fall from a peak, and remember a 50% loss needs a 100% gain to recover. Calmar is CAGR divided by maximum drawdown: return per unit of worst pain. Win rate alone is meaningless. Profit factor, gross profit over gross loss, must exceed 1. And the question that matters most: did it beat simply buying and holding the Nifty? Here every metric was negative and it lost to the index; the exact values are on the card. VectorBT exposes these on the portfolio, and QuantStats builds full tearsheets. Judge a strategy by its worst night, not only its destination.", "The takeaway: Use CAGR across lengths, Sharpe and Sortino for risk-adjusted return, and treat a Sharpe above 3 as suspicious.", "Pause and answer: Why prefer CAGR to total return when comparing tests?", "⏸", "Answer: Total return ignores time; CAGR is fair across lengths."]], ["optimisation-plateaus-beat-peaks", ["Optimisation: plateaus beat peaks. A grid is small lists of parameters, combined with itertools product, dropping invalid pairs where fast is at least slow, with one backtest per combination, then ranked. Every extra value multiplies the work and the false winners. You can vectorise it with 2D entries and exits, one from signals call, pick the best Sharpe with idxmax, and unstack for a heatmap. A broad green plateau is robust; a lone green cell beating its neighbours by a mile, or a winner with very few trades, is luck. On a 16 combination Reliance grid, the best was 5 and 40 at plus 3.10% with 0.1% fees. Adding realistic costs dropped 5 and 30 from 4.23% to 1.76% and changed the winner, and on Gold M most combinations lost. So re optimise per instrument, with costs, then validate out of sample.", "The takeaway: Small grids, invalid combos dropped, and a broad plateau of good parameters beats one sharp peak.", "Pause and answer: Why prefer a plateau to a peak?", "⏸", "Answer: Neighbouring parameters also work, so it's less likely to be luck."]], ["walk-forward-the-only-number-that-counts", ["Walk forward is the only number that counts. Split by time, never shuffle: the first 70% or so in sample, the last 30% out of sample. Tune on in sample only and pick the best Sharpe. Run that single winner on the unseen slice. Then roll it: tune on 250 bars, test the next 100, slide by 100, and repeat, logging picks and results. Check parameter stability, the gap between in and out of sample, and trade counts. On Reliance, with 610 bars split 427 and 183, a 30 and 60 EMA won in sample at plus 15.9% with a Sharpe of 0.71, and then lost 4.57% out of sample. The rolling windows lost 3.30, 6.07 and 4.15%. Expect honest results from simple ideas to look like this, and deflate any winner picked from many tries.", "The takeaway: Split by time, tune in-sample, test the winner on unseen data, and roll forward.", "Pause and answer: Should you shuffle data before splitting?", "⏸", "Answer: Never; split by time."]], ["can-i-trust-this-backtest", ["This ladder asks whether you can trust a promising backtest. One: are signals shifted one bar so trades use only past data? If not, fix look ahead first, with a one bar shift and breakouts compared to the prior bar. Two: are realistic fees and slippage included? If not, add them, because they often flip the winner. Three: does it beat buy and hold or the Nifty on CAGR and drawdown? If not, drop it or rethink, because complexity must earn its keep. Four: does it hold out of sample, with stable parameters and enough trades? If not, treat it as overfit, a lucky peak rather than an edge. If it passes all four, paper trade it in analyze mode, then go small, with a human watching.", "The takeaway: Shifted signals, realistic costs, beating the benchmark, out-of-sample stability and enough trades.", "Pause and answer: First check on any promising backtest?", "⏸", "Answer: Are signals shifted one bar so trades use only past data?"]]], "live": [[null, ["Section nine: going live. Machine learning, streaming and position sizing."]], ["machine-learning-workflow-over-model", ["Machine learning: the workflow matters more than the model. Features are things like RSI, ATR, distance from the EMA and recent returns, with the warm up rows dropped. The label is whether the next bar is up, the close shifted back one compared with today's close. The bar to beat is the base rate: always guessing the majority class. Split by time, with shuffling off, and fit any scaler on the training set only; then walk forward, training on 300 bars, testing on 100, and sliding. Models: logistic regression, a random forest with depth 4 and 100 to 150 trees, and a small neural net with 16 and 8 neurons and scaled inputs; bigger nets fit noise faster. On the Reliance test set, the naive baseline scored 54.4%, logistic regression 47%, random forest 47.7%, and the neural net 49%. None beat the baseline, and walk forward averaged about 49.5%. Expect coin flip accuracy, and treat feature importance as a hint, not proof.", "The takeaway: Features without look-ahead, a next-bar label, a time-ordered split, scalers fit on training only, and beating the base rate.", "Pause and answer: What's the bar a classifier must beat?", "⏸", "Answer: The base rate: always guessing the majority class."]], ["anatomy-of-the-capstone-bot", ["The anatomy of the capstone bot. Fetch daily history. Engineer the features and label. Train, then predict the latest row. Act: buy 1 share at market for delivery on an up signal, and use placesmartorder with a target of 0 to flatten on a down signal. Send an alert via Telegram inside try and except, so a missing link can't crash it. And schedule it after the close with cron, Task Scheduler or OpenAlgo's strategy host. There are gaps to fill before real money: a proper loop, risk checks, sizing, and real logging. Run it in analyze mode, small, with a human watching.", "The takeaway: Fetch, engineer, train, predict the latest row, act via smart orders, alert safely, and schedule after the close.", "Pause and answer: How does the bot exit on a down signal?", "⏸", "Answer: placesmartorder with position_size=0."]], ["websockets-stream-to-watch-rest-to-act", ["This flow shows the rule: stream to watch, REST to act. Connect once. Subscribe to LTP, quote or depth. A callback runs on each tick: it reads the tick, updates state and decides, and it must stay fast. Has a stop, target or trailing level been hit? If not, keep receiving ticks. If yes, send the exit through a REST placeorder call. Then unsubscribe and disconnect.", "The takeaway: Stream ticks through a fast callback, send exits through REST, then unsubscribe and disconnect.", "Pause and answer: Should orders go through the WebSocket?", "⏸", "Answer: No. Stream to watch, REST to act."]], ["streaming-details", ["Streaming details. The client takes an API key, host and WebSocket URL; locally that's ws on 127.0.0.1 port 8765, and in production a secure wss URL from the environment. Instruments are a list of exchange and symbol pairs, and the callback receives a message and reads data, ltp. There are three modes: LTP, the lightest; Quote, with OHLC and volume; and Depth, the order book. Callbacks run on a background thread, so do no downloads or heavy maths inside them, and keep trade state, entry, stop and target, in long lived variables or a class. The server pings about every 30 seconds, and the client reconnects and re subscribes on drops. An example plan: entry 6,000, stop 5,970, target 6,060, a risk to reward of 1 to 2. And a trailing stop: the new stop is the larger of the current stop and the high since entry minus 25.", "The takeaway: Three modes (LTP, Quote, Depth); the local feed is ws://127.0.0.1:8765; callbacks run on another thread.", "Pause and answer: Which streaming mode is lightest?", "⏸", "Answer: LTP."]], ["position-sizing-by-rupee-risk", ["Position sizing by rupee risk. The risk budget is capital times 1%. Quantity is the floor of the budget divided by entry minus stop; round down, and guard against a zero distance. With an ATR stop at entry minus 2 ATR, quantity is the budget divided by 2 ATR. For F and O, units are rounded down to whole lots. And heat, the sum of quantity times entry minus stop over open trades, should stay at or below 6% of capital. Examples: Reliance at 1,400 with a stop at 1,372 risks ₹28 a share; on ₹5 lakh at 1%, ₹5,000, that's 178 shares. A Nifty trade at 25,900 with a stop at 25,750 risks ₹150 a unit; ₹5,000 of risk allows 33 units, which rounds down to zero lots of 65, so the trade is too big for the account. A heat example: Reliance 40 shares risking 28, Infosys 30 risking 30, and Tata Steel 200 risking 4, totals ₹2,820. Correlated positions are one bigger bet, so size them together. Survival maths: with a 55% win rate, risking 2% a trade survives losing streaks that 20% can't, and a 50% loss needs plus 100%. Don't raise size after a winning run, and don't let a tight stop create an oversized position. Pull capital from funds, available cash, and the price from quotes, and wrap it all in one position size helper.", "The takeaway: Qty = floor(1% of capital ÷ stop distance); F&O rounds down to whole lots; keep total heat at or below 6%.", "Pause and answer: How do you round F&O quantity?", "⏸", "Answer: Down to whole lots: (qty // lot_size) × lot_size."]]], "runbook": [[null, ["This section is a personal runbook for actually running a bot with real money. It's written generically, so fill in your own broker, server and numbers, and keep those details private."]], ["costs-per-strategy-on-your-broker", ["Costs per strategy. Fill this table once from your broker's charges page and your own contract notes. Intraday equity pays brokerage on both legs, STT of 0.025 percent on the sell side, exchange and SEBI fees, stamp duty on the buy, and GST. Delivery equity pays STT of 0.1 percent on both buy and sell, plus a DP charge per scrip sold. Index futures pay STT of 0.05 percent on the sell side. Option buyers often find the flat brokerage per order is the biggest cost, with STT of 0.15 percent of premium on the sell side, and STT on intrinsic value if an in the money option is exercised. Option sellers should also count the margin blocked as the real capital cost. Then divide round trip cost by your average edge per trade. If costs eat more than about a third of the edge, a bad day's slippage takes the rest.", "The takeaway: Build a round-trip cost per strategy from your broker's charges and contract notes, then compare it with the average edge per trade.", "Pause and answer: What STT applies to intraday equity, and on which side?", "⏸", "Answer: 0.025% on the sell side."]], ["static-ip-and-algo-id-compliance-steps", ["The compliance steps under SEBI's retail algo framework, mandatory since April 2026. Get a static IP from your internet provider or a cloud server, because API orders are only accepted from a whitelisted static IP. Register it in your broker's developer console, and update it there if it ever changes. Log in with two factor authentication every trading day, since API sessions reset daily, so build a morning login step into your bot. Stay under ten orders per second per exchange segment, or register the strategy through your broker, which gets it an exchange algo ID tagged on every order. Use only platforms your broker has empanelled; you can't connect a script straight to the exchange. And expect market orders to be converted into protected, price capped orders, so handle partial fills. Finally, never publish your server address, keys or broker domain anywhere.", "The takeaway: Whitelisted static IP, daily 2FA login, under 10 orders per second per segment or a registered algo ID, and only broker-empanelled platforms.", "Pause and answer: What happens to API orders from a non-whitelisted IP?", "⏸", "Answer: They are rejected."]], ["sandbox-to-live-a-staged-runbook", ["Going from sandbox to live, in four stages. First, analyze mode: simulated orders on live data, for about two weeks with no unhandled errors and every order path exercised. Second, shadow mode: the bot logs what it would do and you compare it with the market, until simulated fills and costs match the backtest's assumptions. Third, the smallest real size with you watching, until live profit per trade sits within the backtest's expected range after real costs. Fourth, step up to planned size in stages, as long as drawdown stays inside the limit you set beforehand. And before stage three, write down your kill switch: the daily loss, error count or drawdown that stops the bot and flattens positions. Test it in stage one.", "The takeaway: Analyze mode, shadow, smallest real size, then planned size in steps, with a kill switch written and tested before real money.", "Pause and answer: When should the kill switch be defined and tested?", "⏸", "Answer: Defined before the first real-money stage, tested in analyze mode."]], ["my-setup-daily-operating-routine", ["A daily operating routine. Before the open, confirm the server is up and its IP unchanged, log in with two factor authentication, check funds and margin, and make sure the bot's positions match the broker's. During the session, alert on rejected orders, disconnects and the loss limit, and keep the kill switch reachable from your phone. Intraday strategies should stop new entries early and flatten before the broker's square off. After the close, reconcile the bot's log with the tradebook and back up the logs. Weekly, compare live results with the backtest; monthly, recompute costs from contract notes.", "The takeaway: Pre-open checks and 2FA login, alerts in session, flatten before the MIS cutoff, reconcile after the close.", "Pause and answer: What must match before the open?", "⏸", "Answer: The bot's open positions and the broker's positions (plus server IP, funds and margin)."]]], "last": [[null, ["Final section: checklists and quick reference. Read this before the first real money order."]], ["go-live-checklist", ["The go live checklist. Keys live in dot env, never in code or Git. Every call checks its status and survives errors. Signals are shifted one bar, and breakouts use the prior bar's level. The backtest has realistic fees and slippage and beats the benchmark. It holds out of sample, with stable parameters and enough trades. Size comes from rupee risk, rounded down to whole lots, and total heat is capped. Intraday code stops entries before the MIS cutoff and forces flat. Multi leg orders check every leg's status. And you've run it for weeks in analyze mode, with logs and alerts, before going live at small size with a human watching.", "The takeaway: Keys in .env, errors survived, signals shifted, costed benchmark-beating backtest, out-of-sample stability, risk-based size, analyze mode first.", "Pause and answer: Where must keys live before going live?", "⏸", "Answer: In .env, never in code or Git."]], ["idioms-to-keep", ["Idioms to keep. Read resp data, or resp results for batch calls. Returns are close pct change. Equity is the cumulative product of one plus the shifted position times returns. Drawdown is equity over its running max, minus one. Resample OHLCV with first, max, min, last and sum. Wrap a crossover array in a Series with the DataFrame's index. A breakout is today's close above the highest high, compared at minus 2. Annual volatility is daily std times root 252, and Sharpe is mean over std times root 252. And format expiries for orders with strftime, day, short month and two digit year, upper cased.", "The takeaway: resp['data'], pct_change, cumprod equity, cummax drawdown, OHLCV resample, crossover Series, annualised vol.", "Pause and answer: Annual volatility from daily returns?", "⏸", "Answer: Daily std × √252."]], ["about-this-sheet", ["This sheet condenses the free Algo Trading with Python course from OpenAlgo Open Varsity, 32 chapters in 9 modules, built on the OpenAlgo Python SDK, into reference cards in our own words. The numbers come from the course's own runs on mid 2026 data and will differ when you run them, and the code is short illustrative idiom, not copied listings. Each section links back to its chapters. It's educational, not investment advice. That's the sheet. Build carefully, and thanks for listening."]], ["numbers-that-expire", ["The numbers that expire card collects every figure on this sheet that a regulator, exchange or budget can change, like lot sizes, tax rates, STT and expiry days. Each one shows its current value and when it last changed, with a link to the source. Use it as a checklist: before you trade on any number from this sheet, check it there, and then check the source, because these move."]]]};
